(* ============================================================================
  10r. THE MARKDOWN TARGET KEEPS FRONTMATTER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-062]. A
      `frontmatter` node is emitted FIRST, as its own block: a `---` opener
      carrying the format token wherever the format is not `yaml`, the content
      VERBATIM, and a bare `---` closer. HTML, plain text and the terminal omit
      it.

        ---toml            ->  markdown  ---toml
        t = 1                            t = 1
        ---                              ---
        (blank)                          (blank)
        x                                x

      THE SPLIT IS CARVE-P11-028'S TEST, not a new one. HTML may drop an unused
      definition "because HTML has nowhere to put a definition nobody used";
      Markdown has exactly somewhere to put frontmatter, so on this target the
      drop is content loss. Plain text and the terminal are on HTML's side of
      that test here rather than Markdown's: neither reads a delimited metadata
      block, so emitting one leaves raw YAML in prose, which is not
      preservation.

      THE FORMAT TOKEN IS CARVE'S, NOT THE ECOSYSTEM'S. A `toml` body is not
      rewritten to `+++`, and no per-format delimiter table is implied. A
      reader that does not know the fence declines to parse it; one handed a
      TOML body under a bare `---` reads it as YAML and is wrong about the
      values. Declining beats being wrong, and the emitted block re-imports as
      the same node.

      THE CONTENT IS NOT VALIDATED. PART 12 §7 keeps `content` raw because a
      malformed block is still a document's frontmatter; this target emits what
      that node carries without parsing it, so nothing here can fail on a body
      the format's own loader would reject.

  10s. AN OPT-IN MODE CARRIES AN ELEMENT-LESS CONTAINER IN A COMMENT --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-063]. An ELEMENT-LESS CONTAINER is a container
      block this target writes as its children alone: no element, no marker
      and no attribute of its own reaches the output, so nothing there says a
      container was present. Under the CARRIER MODE, which is roundtrip export
      on this target, the target brackets
      each one with an HTML comment carrying the opener and the closer the
      canonical writer spells, widened to the nesting depth, not the author's
      lines: `::::: note   "T"` is carried as `::: note "T"` at the top level.
      CARVE-P10-015 states the option, the table of both
      targets' carriers and the rules they share; this clause and CARVE-P11-067 state
      the marker syntax and what is particular to this target.

        ::: note                 ->  <!-- carve: ::: note -->
        An admonition body.          An admonition body.
        :::                          <!-- carve: ::: -->

      THE SET: a tab set and each of its
      panels (`tabs`, `code-group`), an admonition of any kind whether or not
      it carries a title, a div, named or bare, a columns
      container and each column, a disclosure, a spoiler, a composite
      figure's group wrapper, and a line block (`::: |`) or a hard-break
      block (`::: \`), whose lines this target writes as a paragraph with
      hard breaks. A container this target DOES spell is not one
      and takes no comment: an attributes-only opener (`::: {.warning}`) is
      written verbatim, and a list table is written as a pipe table. A BARE
      DIV'S OPENER IS `:::` WITH NOTHING AFTER IT, so its marker is
      `<!-- carve: ::: -->`, the same bytes as its closer's; no space pads the
      payload.

      THE MARKER IS `<!-- carve: `, the payload, ` -->`. A `-->` the payload
      carries is written `--\>`, and an `\` the payload itself carries before
      such a `>` is written `\\`. Nothing else is escaped: the payload is
      Carve source, so Carve's own escape is the one the reader already has.
      The import reverses exactly those two. A PAYLOAD MAY SPAN LINES: one
      that holds a line break, such as a definition term the canonical writer
      spells over two lines, is written across them, and the marker is the
      whole comment. The import reads it as one marker, with any host prefix
      on its later lines removed by the block structure as for the marker
      itself, and restores the line breaks it holds.

      A MARKER MAY CARRY A DEPTH directly after `carve:`, with no space
      before it: a decimal of 2 or more, as in `<!-- carve:2 : -->`. A bare
      `carve:` is depth 1, so a marker written without a depth keeps its
      meaning. CARVE-P11-067 states which markers carry one. A depth is part
      of the payload, not of the marker's shape: on any other marker it is a
      payload that marker's grammar does not admit, so the marker is invalid
      and drops alone under the rule below, an opener together with its closer.

      A COMPOSITE FIGURE'S CAPTION LINE TAKES A MARKER OF ITS OWN, directly
      after the closer's. The caption slot hangs BELOW the closing fence, so
      the pair bracketing the container cannot enclose it, and carrying it the
      way an attribute line above an opener is carried keeps one rule rather
      than two. The payload is the caption line as the canonical writer
      spells it, `^` and all:

        ::: figure               ->  <!-- carve: ::: figure -->
        :::: panel                   <!-- carve: :::: panel -->
        ![a](x.png)                  ![a](x.png)
        ::::
        :::                          <!-- carve: :::: -->
        ^ Group caption              <!-- carve: ::: -->
                                     <!-- carve: ^ Group caption -->
                                     **Group caption**

      THE IMPORT REPLACES THE CAPTION'S RENDERED PARAGRAPH RATHER THAN
      APPENDING A SECOND ONE. This is the one thing the attribute-line
      precedent does not cover: an attribute line has no rendered form of its
      own, while a caption also renders as body text, so a marker that only
      added the caption line back would leave that paragraph standing beside
      it and the caption would appear twice. The paragraph replaced is the one
      the marker stands directly above and no other, so the same caption text
      appearing as ordinary body text elsewhere in the document is untouched.

      A CAPTION MARKER BELONGS TO THE BLOCK DIRECTLY BEFORE IT, the one rule
      CARVE-P11-067 states for every captioned block; for a composite figure
      that block is the container the marker before it closed. One standing
      above an opener has lost what it recorded, and the set is damaged under
      the rule below. A `^ ...`
      line INSIDE the container is not the caption slot: it is literal text
      that renders as a paragraph, and it takes no marker of its own.

      A MARKER IS READ OFF THE BLOCK STRUCTURE AROUND IT rather than off a scan
      of source lines, so a HOST THAT PREFIXES ITS LINES CARRIES: a comment
      written at a list item's content column or behind a block quote's `>` is
      read back, and the container it brackets is restored in that host. The
      placeholder keeps the host prefix, and the restore removes whatever
      stands before the marker rather than a fixed set of characters, so a
      container opening a list item keeps that item's own marker. A CLOSER
      STANDS AT ITS OPENER'S COLUMN, so a container whose body begins with a
      nested list closes in the host it opened in and not in that inner item.

      A MARKER-SHAPED LINE IN VERBATIM CONTENT RECORDS NO CONTAINER and is
      never read. That narrowing is what the structural read buys: at a list
      item's content column a marker and a verbatim line are not
      distinguishable without the block structure standing around them.

      A TABLE CELL is narrower still, and permanently: this target flattens a
      cell to a single line, so there is no line for a marker to stand on, and
      the container's own body is flattened with it, leaving a restored marker
      nothing to bracket. No read recovers that, so the cell narrowing is
      deliberate.

      THE CARRIER IS A COMMENT, NOT A LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION. An HTML
      comment survives as a node in a Markdown reader, can interrupt a
      paragraph and is not entity-decoded; a `[meta]: # (payload)`
      definition collapses with its duplicates and can capture an author's
      reference. The mode is OPT-IN because a renderer with raw HTML turned
      off shows the comment as text.

      THE ROUND TRIP IS WHAT THE MODE BUYS. A document this target writes
      with the mode on, imported back with `markdown` as the source format,
      returns every container the set names: its kind, its title, its
      attributes, and its nesting, which the import re-derives from the
      block structure. For containers it promises no more than that, and
      CARVE-P11-067 and CARVE-P11-068 name everything else the mode carries. A
      construct none of them carries degrades exactly as it does with the mode
      off, a document
      holding no element-less container gains no comment under either
      setting, and this is not the byte-identical source round trip CARVE-P11-043's
      comparison governs.

      AN INVALID OPENER DROPS TOGETHER WITH ITS CLOSER. Pairing is read on
      marker shapes before payloads are validated, so an opener whose payload
      is not an opener it can stand for still pairs with its closer, and the
      two drop as one unit: both stay ordinary comments and the container's
      content stays in place as ordinary Markdown. A composite figure's
      caption marker belongs to the container that closer ends, so it drops
      with the pair and its caption paragraph stays ordinary text; the set is
      not damaged. Any other marker whose payload is invalid drops alone. The import reports each dropped pair or
      marker once as `carrier-marker-dropped` at the marker's line, as an
      invalid HTML carrier is reported (CARVE-P10-017), and the rest of the set
      restores, since an invalid payload does not break pairing.

      A DAMAGED SET, ONE WHOSE PAIRING BREAKS, IMPORTS AS PLAIN MARKDOWN,
      NEVER A GUESS. A Markdown editor that deleted one marker, reordered two,
      left the set unbalanced or moved a caption marker off the closer it belongs to has
      destroyed the structure the markers recorded, and a reconstruction that
      is wrong is worse than a fallback that is honest.
      The import reads the file as ordinary Markdown, comments and all, and
      reports one `carrier-markers-damaged` diagnostic on the migration
      report (`resources/migration-report-schema.json`) with `degraded`
      fidelity and `fallback` confidence. Only a damaged set falls back as a
      whole.

      WHAT THIS CLAUSE DOES NOT COVER, so a reader does not look for it here.
      A construct whose Markdown output already holds an element needs no
      carrier: `highlight`, `subscript`, `superscript` and a CriticMarkup
      insertion round-trip through `<mark>`, `<sub>`, `<sup>` and `<ins>`
      today. Where such an element does not read back (`<u>`, the
      `critic-comment` span, `<abbr>`, `<del>`), the emitted bytes are
      faithful and the defect is on the import or in CARVE-P11-045, not here.
      `small_caps` and `ruby` have no Carve opener to carry. Attributes on a
      list item's marker line are not carried: CARVE-P11-067 carries an
      attribute LINE, and that is not spelled with one. That is a stated
      limit: the attributes degrade as they do with the mode off, unreported,
      as this target drops any attribute it cannot spell. An inline construct's
      attributes are CARVE-P11-068's.

  10t. THE CARRIER MODE ALSO CARRIES AN ATTRIBUTE LINE, A DEFINITION LIST AND A
      CAPTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-067]. These are not containers, so CARVE-P11-063's
      set does not name them, but each is a distinction this target writes
      without a trace and the AST records. The marker, its escaping, the
      structural read, the verbatim-content and table-cell narrowings and the
      damaged-set rule are CARVE-P11-063's, unchanged; only what each marker stands for
      is new.

      AN ATTRIBUTE LINE THIS TARGET DROPS IS CARRIED DIRECTLY ABOVE ITS
      BLOCK. A block whose authored attributes reach no element of this
      target's output -- a heading's `#id`, a paragraph's class, a block
      quote's, a list's or a table's attributes -- takes one marker on the
      line directly above the block, holding the attribute line as the
      canonical writer spells it. A generated heading id is not authored and
      takes no marker. The import attaches the attributes to the block the
      marker stands directly above and to no other; a marker followed by a
      blank line, by another marker, or by nothing has lost its block and the
      set is damaged.

        {#start}                 ->  <!-- carve: {#start} -->
        # Roundtrip Test             # Roundtrip Test

      A container CARVE-P11-063 brackets already carries its attribute line in the
      opener's prelude and takes no second marker for it.

      A DEFINITION LIST IS CARRIED TERM BY TERM. This target writes each term
      as a paragraph and each definition's blocks after it (CARVE-P11-058), so a term
      takes a marker holding its term line, written directly above the term's
      rendered paragraph, and each definition takes a `:` marker directly
      above its first block. One closing marker with an EMPTY payload follows
      the list's last block:

        :: Carve                 ->  <!-- carve: :: Carve -->
        : A markup language.         **Carve**

                                     <!-- carve: : -->
                                     A markup language.

                                     <!-- carve: -->

      THE IMPORT REPLACES A TERM'S RENDERED PARAGRAPH, as CARVE-P11-063's caption rule
      does, so the term does not appear twice. A definition holds the blocks
      from its marker up to the next term or definition marker of the same
      list, or up to the closing marker. An item's terms are the consecutive
      term markers before its first definition marker, and an item may hold
      no definition at all: a term marker followed by another term marker or
      by the closing marker is a term without a definition, as the AST's
      empty `definitions` records it. A definition marker with no term before
      it, or a list with no closing marker, is a damaged set.

      A NESTED DEFINITION LIST'S MARKERS CARRY ITS DEPTH. This target writes a
      definition's blocks unindented (CARVE-P11-058), so a list nested inside a
      definition writes its term, definition and closing markers with its
      depth (CARVE-P11-063): the outermost list is depth 1, a list inside one
      of its definitions depth 2, and so on. Markers pair by depth: the
      "markers of the same list" above are the markers of the same depth. A
      marker of depth 2 or more with no open list of the depth one less around
      it breaks the pairing, and the set is damaged. Only term, definition
      and closing markers carry a depth; a depth on any other marker, such as
      `<!-- carve:2 ::: note -->` or `<!-- carve:2 {.x} -->`, makes that marker
      invalid. It drops alone, an opener together with its closer under the
      invalid-opener rule above, and is reported once as
      `carrier-marker-dropped` (CARVE-P11-063).

        <!-- carve: :: Outer -->
        <!-- carve: : -->
        <!-- carve:2 :: Inner -->
        <!-- carve:2 : -->
        <!-- carve:2 -->
        <!-- carve: -->

      A DEFINITION LIST'S OWN ATTRIBUTE LINE stands directly above its first
      term marker, as that list's prelude, the way a container's attribute
      line precedes its opener. That one adjacency is not "a marker followed
      by another marker": an attribute marker directly above a list's first
      term marker attaches to the list. A nested list's attribute line is
      carried the same way, and an attribute marker carries no depth.

      A CAPTION ON ANY CAPTIONED BLOCK TAKES THE COMPOSITE FIGURE'S CAPTION
      MARKER. A block quote's attribution, a table's, an image's, a code
      listing's or an equation's `^ ...` line is written as a paragraph after
      the block (CARVE-P11-032); under the carrier mode a marker holding the caption
      line as the canonical writer spells it stands directly above that paragraph, and the import
      replaces the paragraph with the caption, exactly as CARVE-P11-063 rules for a
      composite figure:

        > Stay hungry.           ->  > Stay hungry.
        ^ Steve Jobs
                                     <!-- carve: ^ Steve Jobs -->
                                     Steve Jobs

      A CAPTION MARKER BELONGS TO THE BLOCK DIRECTLY BEFORE IT. One with no
      captionable block before it, or one whose block already holds a caption,
      is a damaged set.

      A CODE FENCE'S TITLE AND LABEL TAKE A FENCE MARKER. This target writes
      both into the info string, where a Markdown reader sees free text and
      the import drops it (CARVE-P2-029). Under the carrier mode a fenced code
      block with a title or a label takes one marker directly above its fence,
      holding the opener as the canonical writer spells it. The import
      restores the title and the label from that opener onto the code block
      directly below, and nothing else; the fence itself is written as
      without the mode.

        ```js "app.js" [Example]  ->  <!-- carve: ```js "app.js" [Example] -->
        let x = 1                     ```js "app.js" [Example]
        ```                           let x = 1
                                      ```

      A title is not an attribute, so it is not carried as `{title=...}`. A
      block's attribute marker stands directly above its fence marker, as a
      definition list's does above its first term marker. A fence marker
      not directly above a fenced code block is a damaged set. Without the
      mode, a title with a language stays in the info string and reports
      nothing. A title without a language reports `fence-title-dropped`
      (CARVE-P2-024); the label stays in the info string.

      A LIST'S NUMBERING STYLE TAKES A LIST-STYLE MARKER. This target writes
      an alphabetic or roman ordered list as a decimal list (CARVE-P2-024), so
      under the carrier mode such a list takes one marker directly above its
      first item, holding the first item's marker as the canonical writer
      spells it. The import takes the style from that marker and the start
      and delimiter from the Markdown list, so a marker supplies the style
      only. A list-style marker not directly above an ordered list is a
      damaged set.

        a. one                   ->  <!-- carve: a. -->
        a. two                       1. one
                                     2. two

      AN AMBIGUOUS FIRST LETTER TAKES TWO MARKERS. When the first item's
      marker is a letter that is also a roman numeral (`i`, `v`, `x`, `l`,
      `c`, `d`, `m`, in either case), one marker cannot tell an alphabetic
      list from a roman one, so the list-style marker holds the first two
      item markers, separated by one space, and the import applies the
      ambiguous-letter tie-break (PART 9 §11 N3) to them as Carve source
      does. A single-item list with such a first marker holds its one
      marker and takes the reading N3 gives a lone first marker. Every other
      list keeps the one-marker form.

        i. one                   ->  <!-- carve: i. j. -->
        j. two                       9. one
                                     10. two

        i. one                   ->  <!-- carve: i. ii. -->
        ii. two                      1. one
                                     2. two

      A list's attribute marker stands directly above its list-style
      marker, as a block's does above its fence marker, and the import reads
      the two as one stack: the style from the list-style marker, the
      attributes from the attribute marker. A block between the two breaks
      the stack, and each marker is read on its own.

        {.steps}                 ->  <!-- carve: {.steps} -->
        a. one                       <!-- carve: a. -->
        b. two                       1. one
                                     2. two

  10u. THE CARRIER MODE CARRIES AN INLINE CONSTRUCT'S ATTRIBUTES, A SPAN AND
      A CROSS-REFERENCE IN INLINE MARKERS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-068].
      Markdown has no spelling for a link's, an image's or a span's
      attributes, none for a span, and none for a cross-reference, which this
      target writes as a link (CARVE-P11-038). Under the carrier mode the
      output stays exactly what this target writes without it, and an inline
      marker in CARVE-P11-063's syntax and escaping is added beside the
      construct. Each payload is Carve source as the canonical writer spells
      it:

        [l](u){.k}         ->  [l](u)<!-- carve: {.k} -->
        ![a](i.png){w=3}   ->  ![a](i.png)<!-- carve: {w=3} -->
        [x]{.c}            ->  <!-- carve: [ -->x<!-- carve: ]{.c} -->
        </#h>              ->  <!-- carve: </#h> -->[Head](#head)

      AN ATTRIBUTE MARKER STANDS DIRECTLY AFTER ITS LINK OR IMAGE, however
      the link is spelled, and the import attaches its attributes to that
      construct. A SPAN TAKES AN OPENER `[` AND A CLOSER `]` FOLLOWED BY ITS
      ATTRIBUTE BLOCK, around the content this target writes for it; the
      pairs nest with the spans. A CROSS-REFERENCE MARKER STANDS DIRECTLY
      BEFORE THE LINK this target writes for a resolved cross-reference, whose
      fragment stays the GFM slug, and the import replaces that link with
      the cross-reference it names. An unresolved one takes no marker.

      AN INLINE MARKER IS TEXT TO NO READER. It contributes nothing to a GFM
      slug (CARVE-P11-038) or to an image's description, and an image's
      description holds no marker. A table cell and verbatim content take
      none, as for CARVE-P11-063's markers.

      AN INVALID MARKER DROPS ALONE (CARVE-P11-063): a payload outside these
      shapes, an attribute marker not directly after a link or image, and a
      cross-reference marker not directly before a link are ordinary comments
      and are reported. A span closer without its opener, or an opener left
      unclosed in its paragraph, breaks the pairing, and the set is damaged.

  11. THE MARKDOWN TARGET LINKS A HEADING BY ITS GFM SLUG --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-038]. GFM has no heading-id syntax: a reader derives every
      heading's id from its text, and `## H {#h}` shows the suffix as text.
      So this target writes no `{#id}` suffix. A link or a resolved
      cross-reference whose fragment names a heading is written with that
      heading's GFM SLUG as its fragment:

        {#h}
        ## Rise and divergence     ->  ## Rise and divergence

        See </#h>.                 ->  See [Rise and divergence](#rise-and-divergence).

      The carrier mode keeps this link and its slug, and adds CARVE-P11-068's
      marker before it so the import restores `</#h>`.

      THE SLUG is the one GitHub assigns (github-slugger) and `pandoc -f gfm`
      reads:

      G1 TEXT: the heading's text content under PART 2 HEADING IDENTIFIERS'
         contribution rule, each character as this target writes it (a smart
         punctuation glyph as the glyph, its source under source typography),
         NFC-normalized, with leading and trailing whitespace removed. A hard
         break contributes no text here: CARVE-P11-065 writes `<br>`, whose text content
         is empty. A soft break contributes the heading's folded SPACE. PART 2
         counts an image's alt text and GitHub does not, so a heading holding
         an image can get a slug GitHub does not assign; PART 2's rule is kept
         so that every engine derives the text one way;
      G2 lowercased (Unicode default case mapping);
      G3 with every character removed that is not a letter, a mark, a number,
         connector punctuation (`_`), U+0020 SPACE or `-`;
      G4 with each SPACE replaced by `-`;
      G5 DEDUPLICATED over every heading the target writes, linked or not, in
         the order it writes them. Each base keeps a count starting at 0. A
         base not yet taken is the slug. Otherwise the count is raised by one
         and `-` plus the count appended, repeating while the result is
         taken; the result is then taken too.

      THE REFERENCE IS STILL RECOGNIZED BY THE ID THE DOCUMENT ASSIGNED. A
      fragment names a heading when it equals that heading's id, explicit or
      derived under PART 2; only the WRITTEN destination is the slug. A
      heading the target does not write as a heading (one flattened into a
      table cell) is not a target, and a fragment naming no heading is CARVE-P11-049's.

      NO ANCHOR CAN DANGLE. The target writes no anchor at all, and the
      reader derives each slug from text the target wrote, so every link to a
      heading resolves in a GFM reader (carve#352). A heading whose text
      removes to nothing has the empty slug, and a link to it is written `#`,
      or `#-1` and so on for a repeat.

      A CROSS-REFERENCE CONTRIBUTES NOTHING TO THE TEXT, as PART 2 says: by the
      time this target runs, resolution has replaced `</#a>` with a link
      carrying the target heading's text, and counting it would slug
      `# A </#a>` as `a-a`.

      THE SCAN INCLUDES FOOTNOTE DEFINITION BODIES. They render as block
      content, so a heading or a reference inside one is a heading or a
      reference.

  11a. THE MARKDOWN TARGET KEEPS A FRAGMENT LINK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P11-049].
      A `link` whose destination is a fragment (`#id`) is emitted as a Markdown
      link whether or not `id` names a heading. The author wrote the
      destination, so it is content: writing the label alone drops it, and a
      reader of the Markdown loses a link the HTML target keeps.

        {#h}
        ## Head                   ->  ## Head

        See [head](#h), [three](#chap03) and [gone](#nowhere).
                                  ->  See [head](#head), [three](#chap03) and [gone](#nowhere).

      A FRAGMENT THAT NAMES NO HEADING ADDS NO ANCHOR ANYWHERE: the target
      writes no ids, so `#chap03` above points at an id the Markdown does not
      carry. The link is kept anyway, because a page that embeds the Markdown
      can carry the id, and dropping the destination cannot be undone.

      THE DESTINATION IS WRITTEN LIKE ANY OTHER. A fragment that names a
      heading is the heading's GFM slug (§11); any other fragment is the
      authored destination under the ordinary destination encoding, `#`
      alone included.

      THE LABEL IS WRITTEN LIKE ANY OTHER LINK LABEL. `[[1]](#n1)` is emitted
      as `[\[1\]](#n1)`, with both text brackets escaped inside the label.

      A CROSS-REFERENCE IS OUTSIDE THIS CLAUSE. §11 still writes a resolved
      cross-reference to a target that is not a heading as its display text;
      this clause changes only the `link` node.

   ============================================================================ *)
